=== HINT 1: mmap anonymous RW ===
void *ptr = mmap(NULL, 4096,
                 PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                 MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS,
                 -1, 0);

=== HINT 2: Copy shellcode ===
memcpy(ptr, ret_stub, sizeof(ret_stub));

=== HINT 3: mprotect to RX ===
mprotect(ptr, 4096, PROT_READ | PROT_EXEC);

=== HINT 4: Execute ===
void (*fn)(void) = (void(*)(void))ptr;
fn();
printf("returned from shellcode\n");

=== HINT 5: Unmap ===
munmap(ptr, 4096);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
W^X (write XOR execute) is enforced by NX hardware + kernel.
Never mmap with PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC - that's RWX and triggers seccomp/SELinux alerts.
Correct staging: mmap RW → write payload → mprotect RX → execute.
On Linux, mprotect on anonymous mappings generally works without privileges.
On hardened systems (grsecurity, SELinux deny_execmem), mprotect(PROT_EXEC) on
anonymous mappings is blocked - use a memfd + MAP_SHARED workaround instead.
